koto-verbs-note
---------------

(Modelled on the Linux kernel's Linux-syscall-note.)

   NOTE! This copyright does *not* cover programs that use koto services
 through its verbs - this is merely considered normal use of koto, and
 does *not* fall under the heading of "derived work". The verbs are:

   - the gRPC API defined in protocol/koto.proto, including the definitions
     in protocol/guest.proto and code generated from either file, such as
     the generated code in protocol/pb;
   - the `koto ctl` command line;
   - the in-guest control plane: the ctl FIFO and its verbs, the guest-side
     tools (cs-job, cs-notify, cs-subagent) and the environment a turn
     exposes to the programs it runs.

   Likewise, agents, prompts, scripts and other workloads that run inside a
 koto group are not derived works of koto merely because koto runs them.

   This note is an additional permission from the copyright holders. It
 applies under whichever version of the GNU General Public License you
 use koto under.

   Also note that the GPL is copyrighted by the Free Software Foundation,
 but the instance of code that it refers to (koto) is copyrighted by the
 people who actually wrote it.


Why "or later"
--------------

koto's binaries statically link Apache-2.0 modules (grpc, genproto, gVisor,
gvisor-tap-vsock, google/btree, inetaf/tcpproxy). Apache-2.0 is compatible
with version 3 of the GPL but not with version 2 alone, so a binary that
combines them is distributed under the terms of GPL version 3 or later,
which the grant above permits. koto's own source remains available under
version 2 or later.


Third-party components
----------------------

Third-party code keeps its own license: tui/themes/ (MIT, see
tui/themes/LICENSE), the Go modules compiled into koto, and Firecracker,
the guest kernel and the guest root filesystem, which ship alongside koto
as separate programs. The pinned versions are in the go.mod files and in
fcguest/build-firecracker.sh, build-kernel.sh and Dockerfile.rootfs.
